  "assumptions": [
    "Idealized model: small swings, period T = 2π√(L/g) with g = 9.81 m/s²; this is exact for the model and only approximately true of a real pendulum.",
    "The string is light and doesn't stretch, and the bob is treated as a single point at the end of the string.",
    "There is no air resistance or friction, so the swing never dies down.",
    "The swing amplitude is a fixed 10°. Real periods at large angles are slightly longer; that is not shown.",
    "The swing is computed from the timeline time, so changing the length restarts the motion from the current time, and the swing may jump when the 60 s timeline loops."
  ],
  "program": {
    "source": "export function evaluate({ inputs, time }) {\n  const L = inputs.length;\n  const g = 9.81;\n  const T = 2 * Math.PI * Math.sqrt(L / g);\n  const amp = 10 * Math.PI / 180;\n  const th = amp * Math.cos(2 * Math.PI * time / T);\n  return {\n    nodes: {\n      arm: { quaternion: [0, 0, Math.sin(th / 2), Math.cos(th / 2)] },\n      stringN: { position: [0, -L / 2, 0], scale: [1, L, 1] },\n      bobN: { position: [0, -L, 0] }\n    },\n    readouts: { period: T, len: L }\n  };\n}\n"
  }
